        <h3 id="customer-services">Part B – Services the Customer chooses (not coolLabs Sub-processors)</h3>
        <p>The Customer may connect its own third-party services to Coolify Cloud. These services are engaged and controlled by the Customer. They act as the Customer’s own processors or as independent controllers. They are <strong>not</strong> Sub-processors of coolLabs, and the Customer is responsible for having appropriate agreements with them. Examples:</p>
        <ul>
        <li>the Customer’s own servers and hosting/cloud providers (Customer Servers);</li>
        <li>Git providers (for example GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket, Gitea);</li>
        <li>OAuth / identity providers used for sign-in;</li>
        <li>the Customer’s own Cloudflare account (for example Cloudflare Tunnels) and DNS providers;</li>
        <li>S3-compatible storage for backups configured by the Customer;</li>
        <li>notification targets such as Discord, Telegram, Slack, Pushover, webhooks or the Customer’s own SMTP or email provider;</li>
        <li>container registries and any other integrations the Customer configures.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Coolify Cloud sends data to these services only when the Customer configures it to do so.</p>
        </section>
        <section id="annex-iv">
        <h2>Annex IV – International transfers (SCCs)</h2>
        <ol>
        <li><strong>No transfer to coolLabs.</strong> coolLabs is established in Hungary and hosts the control plane in Germany. coolLabs personnel with production access work from the EEA. A Customer in the EEA therefore makes no restricted transfer when it uses Coolify Cloud.</li>
        <li><strong>Onward transfers to Sub-processors.</strong> Some Sub-processors process Customer Personal Data outside the EEA (see Annex III). For each such transfer, coolLabs relies on the transfer mechanism in that Sub-processor’s data processing agreement: the EU–U.S. Data Privacy Framework where the Sub-processor is certified, and otherwise the SCCs (Module Three, Processor to Processor). The clause options of those SCCs, including governing law and courts, are set in the Sub-processor’s agreement.</li>
        <li><strong>Customers outside the EEA.</strong> If the Customer is established outside the EEA and Applicable Data Protection Law requires a transfer tool for Customer Personal Data that coolLabs sends to the Customer, the SCCs (Module Four, Processor to Controller) apply between coolLabs as data exporter and the Customer as data importer, with these options:<ul><li>Clause 7 (docking clause) does not apply;</li><li>Clause 11 optional language does not apply;</li><li>Clauses 17 and 18: Hungarian law and the courts of Hungary;</li><li>Annexes I and II of the SCCs are completed by Annexes I and II of this DPA.</li></ul></li>
        <li><strong>Requests from public authorities.</strong> If a public authority asks coolLabs for Customer Personal Data, coolLabs will: (a) examine whether the request is lawful and challenge it where there are reasonable grounds; (b) ask the authority to send the request to the Customer where possible; (c) tell the Customer about the request without undue delay, unless the law prohibits this; and (d) disclose only the minimum data that the request legally requires.</li>
        <li><strong>Transfer reviews.</strong> coolLabs reviews the transfer mechanisms of its Sub-processors when it adds a Sub-processor and when the law changes. Where needed, it carries out a transfer impact assessment and puts supplementary measures in place.</li>
        <li>The full text of the SCCs is not reproduced in this DPA.</li>
        </ol>
